# Kerbin

Free to use Gemini-to-HTTP proxy that lets you read public HTTP and HTTPS web pages and images in your Gemini client.

=> https://github.com/marty1885/kerbin Kerbin source code.

## Using Kerbin with Lagrange

1. Open Preferences, then Proxies (on mobile, the Network panel), and set the HTTP proxy field to the address below.

```
kerbin.northwire.xyz:1964
```

2. Save, then open any ordinary http:// or https:// URL in Lagrange! And you should be able to open HTTP links, including the following!

=> https://example.com/ Try reading a web page through Kerbin

Please read the privacy policy before using Kerbin. As a protocol translation proxy, Kerbin will have access to the raw transmitted information being transmitted. Though we do out best to never look at it.

=> gemini://kerbin.northwire.xyz/privacy.gmi Privacy policy

## Using other Gemini clients with Kerbin

Kerbin interperts a Gemini request with a HTTP(S) URL as a proxy request. Most Gemini clients with HTTP proxying should support this scheme, but check your client before use. To be specific, Kerbin expects the following text wrapped in TLS:

```Proxy requesu example
https://example.com/\r\n
```

## Limitations

* Best effort translation from HTML to Gemtext
* JavaScript, sign-ins, and form submissions are not supported
* Kerbin does not strictly follow HTTP caching headers to prevent potentiall abuse
* Very large or complex pages may be refused

## Known issues

Fetching https://www.fsf.org/ currently fails because its TLS cipher suites are incompatible with Kerbin's TLS backend. We are aware of the issue.
